# Copy to .env.local and fill in. .env.local is gitignored; this file is committed.

# --- Database (local Docker Postgres 18 from Plan 01) -----------------------
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://fitout:fitout@localhost:5432/fitout

# TEST_DATABASE_URL — OPTIONAL. Leave it UNSET; that is the normal path.
# The vitest suite runs against its OWN database so that stray writes through the app's module-level
# db singleton (src/lib/db/index.ts) can never reach dev data. With this unset, the suite derives
# `<DATABASE_URL's database>_test` (i.e. `fitout_test`) automatically. Provision it once with:
#   npm run db:test:setup
# Set this ONLY to point the suite at some other Postgres. HARD REQUIREMENT: the database name must
# end in `_test` — the suite TRUNCATEs every table in the target's public schema on each run and
# REFUSES to start against any other name (tests/helpers/test-db-url.ts).
# Note this affects `npx vitest run` ONLY. `db:migrate`, `db:seed`, `next dev` and the Playwright e2e
# specs all still read DATABASE_URL and still target DEV.
# TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://fitout:fitout@localhost:5432/fitout_test

# DIRECT_DATABASE_URL — OPTIONAL. Leave it UNSET; that is the normal path, and UNSET IS CORRECT for
# all local Docker work. It exists only for a hosted Postgres (Neon) deployment and does nothing at
# all otherwise: with it unset, drizzle.config.ts resolves exactly what it resolved before this
# variable existed.
# WHY A SECOND URL AT ALL. Neon hands out TWO connection strings per branch whose hostnames differ by
# exactly ONE segment — the POOLED host contains `-pooler`, the DIRECT host does not. Nothing else
# about the two strings differs, so they are trivially easy to mix up. Check for `-pooler` before you
# paste. They are NOT a fallback pair for one value; they name two different endpoints on purpose:
#   DATABASE_URL         -> the POOLED host. This is what the APP RUNTIME (src/lib/db/index.ts) reads.
#                           Serverless instances multiply under load and the pooler is what absorbs
#                           that, so the running app must go through it.
#   DIRECT_DATABASE_URL  -> the DIRECT host, for OUT-OF-BAND TOOLING. Transaction-mode pooling does not
#                           hold the session-scoped state schema migrations need. `npm run db:migrate`,
#                           `npm run db:generate` and `npm run db:studio` pick this up automatically
#                           via drizzle.config.ts once it is set.
# COVERAGE IS NOT BLANKET. `npm run db:seed` and `npm run db:test:setup` read DATABASE_URL themselves
# (scripts/seed.ts, scripts/db-test-setup.ts) and are NOT covered by the drizzle.config.ts change.
# Against Neon, hand them the direct URL explicitly on the command line instead:
#   DATABASE_URL="$DIRECT_DATABASE_URL" npm run db:seed
# Do not "fix" that by editing those scripts.
# MIGRATIONS DO NOT RUN THEMSELVES. No build step anywhere invokes `db:migrate` — only CI jobs do, each
# against its own throwaway service container — so a freshly created Neon database is EMPTY until an
# operator runs `npm run db:migrate` ONCE, by hand, against it. Do NOT wire `db:migrate` into the
# Vercel build to solve that: every preview deploy would then re-run migrations against one shared
# database.
# `prepare: false` IS DELIBERATELY NOT SET on the app's postgres.js client, and its absence is not an
# oversight to correct. Neon's pooler runs PgBouncer 1.22+, which supports PROTOCOL-LEVEL prepared
# statements, so the familiar Supabase/PgBouncer "disable prepared statements" advice does not apply
# here. src/lib/db/index.ts is intentionally optionless: long comments in src/lib/db/schema.ts and
# src/lib/payments/checkout-lease.ts reason from postgres.js's DEFAULT pool size, and adding client
# options there would falsify both.
# DIRECT_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@db-direct-host.example.invalid:5432/fitout?sslmode=require

# --- Better Auth core ------------------------------------------------------
# BETTER_AUTH_SECRET: any random >= 32-char string. Generate with:
#   openssl rand -base64 32   (or any password generator). Used to sign/encrypt sessions.
# REQUIRED in production: src/lib/auth.ts THROWS at boot if this is missing when
# NODE_ENV=production (fail-closed — refuses to run on a weak/default secret). In dev/test a
# missing value is tolerated (Better Auth's dev default) so local setup is frictionless.
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=
# Base URL of the app — OAuth callbacks, email links, and CSRF/trustedOrigins are built from this.
# Set this to your real deployed origin in production (e.g. https://app.fitout.com).
BETTER_AUTH_URL=http://localhost:3000
# The SAME origin again, for the browser bundle. THESE TWO MUST MATCH.
#
# Why a second name at all: BETTER_AUTH_URL is server-only, and `metadataBase` in
# src/app/layout.tsx is evaluated where a NEXT_PUBLIC_ variable is the one that is inlined. Next
# inlines NEXT_PUBLIC_* into the client bundle at BUILD time, so this must be set when you build,
# not merely when you run.
#
# WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU SKIP IT (WR-06 of the phase-10 re-review): layout.tsx silently pins
# metadataBase to http://localhost:3000, and Next does NOT warn — setting metadataBase at all is
# precisely what suppresses its missing-metadataBase warning. So a deploy that sets BETTER_AUTH_URL
# and nothing else, which is the shape every other document here points at, ships production Open
# Graph and canonical URLs pointing at localhost with nothing red anywhere.
#
# Must be an absolute http(s) URL. A scheme-less `fitout.ph:443` PARSES but has a null origin and
# cannot have a relative URL resolved against it; layout.tsx rejects it and falls back to localhost.
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=http://localhost:3000

# --- FitOut Ops host ------------------------------------------------------
# Server-only exact origin for the staff console and its sign-in/invitation journey. This value is
# intentionally NOT NEXT_PUBLIC_*: Proxy, Better Auth, emails and server-rendered cross-host links
# consume it on the server. Production must set the real dedicated origin (for example
# https://ops.fitout.com). Local development can use the hostname below without a hosts-file entry.
# The hostname must differ from BETTER_AUTH_URL/NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL; suffixes and wildcard preview
# domains are never accepted as the ops surface.
OPS_APP_URL=http://ops.localhost:3000

# --- Google OAuth (Plan 02 — enables "Sign in with Google", D-06) ----------
# Google Cloud Console -> APIs & Services -> Credentials -> Create OAuth 2.0
# Client ID (type: Web application). Add this Authorized redirect URI:
#   http://localhost:3000/api/auth/callback/google
# Email/password sign-in works WITHOUT these; only Google sign-in needs them.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# --- Resend transactional email (Plan 02+ — verification + password reset) --
# Resend Dashboard -> API Keys. OPTIONAL in dev: when RESEND_API_KEY is unset,
# src/lib/email.ts logs the verify/reset link to the console ([email:dev] ...)
# instead of sending a real email — sufficient for local testing.
RESEND_API_KEY=
EMAIL_FROM=FitOut <onboarding@resend.dev>

# --- Cloudinary avatar upload (Plan 04 — avatar upload/delivery, D-11) ------
# Cloudinary Dashboard -> Account Details (cloud name) and API Keys (key/secret).
# CLOUDINARY_API_SECRET is SERVER-ONLY — never expose it to the client.
# Profiles work without an avatar (avatar is optional); only avatar upload needs these.
CLOUDINARY_CLOUD_NAME=
CLOUDINARY_API_KEY=
CLOUDINARY_API_SECRET=

# --- PayMongo marketplace payments (Plan 06 — host onboarding + payout gate, D-20) ----
# PayMongo is a BSP-regulated PH EMI (QRPh + GCash + Maya + cards). There is NO official SDK —
# Plan 06 builds a thin server-side `fetch` wrapper (src/lib/paymongo.ts) that reads these.
# The secret key is SERVER-ONLY (Platforms/Linked-Accounts + /v2/batch_transfers payouts) —
#   never expose it to the client; it must never carry a NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix.
# The public (publishable) key is client-safe (used by the client-side payment element).
# The webhook secret verifies the `Paymongo-Signature` header (HMAC-SHA256 over the raw body)
#   on the `merchant.activated` onboarding + payment/payout webhooks (the bookability gate source of truth).
# No country env — PayMongo is PH-native (D-19 `country=US` superseded by D-20).
PAYMONGO_SECRET_KEY=
PAYMONGO_PUBLIC_KEY=
PAYMONGO_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# NEXT_PUBLIC_CLOUDINARY_CLOUD_NAME: the cloud name is ALSO needed client-side by CldUploadWidget
# (listing photo step, Plan 04). Same value as CLOUDINARY_CLOUD_NAME; safe to expose. Without it the
# wizard photo step throws at render.
NEXT_PUBLIC_CLOUDINARY_CLOUD_NAME=
# NEXT_PUBLIC_CLOUDINARY_API_KEY: the API KEY is also needed client-side by CldUploadWidget for SIGNED
# uploads (public-safe — it only identifies the account; the API SECRET stays server-only). Same
# value as CLOUDINARY_API_KEY.
NEXT_PUBLIC_CLOUDINARY_API_KEY=

# --- Payments config + Inngest (Phase 5 — payout sweep + reconcile) ---------
# Commission/payout mechanism defaults (Phase 7 can tune without touching plumbing).
COMMISSION_RATE_BPS=1000
PAYOUT_DELAY_HOURS=24
PAYMENT_WINDOW_MINUTES=60
# Request-to-book (Phase 6, D-64) — host approval SLA + post-approval payment window, both in HOURS and
# both tunable. A `requested` hold auto-declines after APPROVAL_SLA_HOURS; an `approved` (but unpaid) hold
# auto-releases after APPROVAL_PAYMENT_WINDOW_HOURS. Nothing is ever charged until the booker pays.
APPROVAL_SLA_HOURS=24
# ⚠️ WARNING (D-95): if APPROVAL_PAYMENT_WINDOW_HOURS=24 is set in a deployed environment, the 12h default
# will NOT take effect. Unset it. The post-approval payment window moved 24h → 12h in Phase 7 (D-95 partially
# supersedes D-64); an env var left at 24 silently keeps the old policy.
APPROVAL_PAYMENT_WINDOW_HOURS=12
# PAYOUT_RECONCILE_STUCK_HOURS: threshold past which a still-Processing transfer raises a
#   stuck-row operator alert (consumed by the reconcile cron in Plan 05b).
PAYOUT_RECONCILE_STUCK_HOURS=48
# Platform payout wallet (source_account for inhouse /v2/batch_transfers). BIC defaults to PAEYPHM2XXX.
PLATFORM_WALLET_NUMBER=
PLATFORM_WALLET_NAME=
PLATFORM_WALLET_BIC=PAEYPHM2XXX
# Inngest (managed cron for the payout sweep + reconcile). Dev server needs no keys;
#   prod: the /api/inngest serve() endpoint (Plan 05b) verifies requests via INNGEST_SIGNING_KEY.
INNGEST_EVENT_KEY=
INNGEST_SIGNING_KEY=

# --- Phase 7: cancellation, service fee, expiry guards, reminders -----------
# Every value below has a working default in src/lib/payments/config.ts — set one here ONLY to override.
# All are commented out on purpose so the code default is the single source of truth.
#
# ⚠️ WARNING (D-95): if APPROVAL_PAYMENT_WINDOW_HOURS=24 is set in a deployed environment, the 12h default
# will NOT take effect. Unset it. (The variable itself lives in the Phase-5/6 block above.)
#
# D-74/D-76 booker-facing service fee in basis points (500 = 5%), computed on the SPACE price.
# SERVICE_FEE_BPS=500
# D-71 flat host-cancellation fee in centavos (30000 = PHP 300), capped at the booking value at write time.
# HOST_CANCEL_FEE_CENTS=30000
# D-93/D-96 minimum lead time to CREATE a request-to-book (hours) — request mode ONLY.
# MIN_LEAD_REQUEST_HOURS=2
# D-96 minimum lead time to CREATE an instant-book hold (minutes) — a checkout-sized guard.
# MIN_LEAD_INSTANT_MINUTES=30
# D-93/D-96 minimum remaining window below which an APPROVE is refused; also the D-96 split floor (hours).
# MIN_APPROVE_WINDOW_HOURS=1
# D-85/D-87 reminder offsets, in hours before the relevant deadline. One reminder each, ever.
# PRE_EXPIRY_REMINDER_HOURS=4
# PRE_SLA_REMINDER_HOURS=6
# PRE_SESSION_BOOKER_REMINDER_HOURS=24
# PRE_SESSION_HOST_REMINDER_HOURS=12

# --- Ops alerting (quick 260810-j3z — the daily unresolved-needs_attention digest) ---
# OPS_ALERT_EMAIL: where the daily digest of UNRESOLVED `needs_attention` money alerts is sent
#   (src/inngest/functions/ops-alert-digest.ts, daily 08:50 Asia/Manila). UNSET = the cron logs
#   loudly and no-ops; it deliberately does NOT throw (a missing notification address must not take
#   down the whole /api/inngest serve mount — contrast INNGEST_SIGNING_KEY, which is a security key
#   and DOES fail closed at boot). The digest carries audit id / action / actor_id / created_at / age
#   and NEVER the `meta` column (D-72 PII contract).
#   A day with ZERO unresolved rows sends NOTHING — that silence is the design, not a failure.
OPS_ALERT_EMAIL=ops@example.com
# OPS_ALERT_AGING_HOURS: age past which an unresolved alert is flagged AGING in the digest.
#   Default 24 — the digest is daily, so anything that appears twice is aging.
OPS_ALERT_AGING_HOURS=24

# --- Didit identity verification (Phase 18.1 — host KYC behind the port, D-258) ----
# The vendor that answers "is this host a real, identified person?". FitOut is a PORT to it and is
# NEVER a custodian of identity documents: the ID, the selfie and the liveness capture stay on
# Didit, and `host_verification` keeps only { result, vendorRef, checkedAt, provider }.
#
# ⚠ SANDBOX AND LIVE ARE SEPARATE APPLICATIONS WITH SEPARATE KEYS, and their traffic never mixes.
#   The test hook (`sandbox_scenario`) is REJECTED by a live application, and that rejection is the
#   guarantee a test hook can never auto-approve a real host — so WHICH KEY sits in .env.local is
#   what decides which world you are in. The Console's "V1 LIVE" badge is the WORKFLOW VERSION being
#   published, NOT the application mode; the two look alike and only a `sandbox_scenario` probe tells
#   them apart. Every verdict arrives later by webhook — the create call never returns one.
#
# All three values are read on the SERVER SIDE ONLY and none may ever carry a NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix.
# NOTHING WAS INSTALLED FOR THIS. There is no official SDK and none is wanted; the adapter is a thin
#   `fetch` wrapper, the same shape as src/lib/paymongo.ts. (`didit-sdk` on npm is a DIFFERENT
#   product — a Web3 login protocol — and is a recorded REMOVED finding, not a candidate.)
# RECONCILE THE ACCOUNT rather than trusting it: `npm run didit:verify` is read-only and prints the
#   values it parsed; `npm run didit:apply` creates what is missing, never edits what exists, and
#   writes the two values it mints STRAIGHT INTO .env.local rather than to stdout — a scrollback, a
#   CI log and an agent transcript are all places a shared secret must not land.
# ⚠ `neon link` / `neon deploy` OVERWRITE .env.local — always pass `--no-env-pull`.
#
# DIDIT_API_KEY: Didit Console -> Application -> API keys. Authenticates every outbound call (the
#   `x-api-key` header). A missing, malformed, expired or wrong-application key ALL answer HTTP 403
#   with no machine-readable discriminator, so any 401/403 is one credential fault — never branch on
#   which, and never surface it to a host.
DIDIT_API_KEY=
# DIDIT_WORKFLOW_ID: Didit Console -> Workflows -> the workflow's UUID. Its composition is exactly
#   ID Verification + Liveness + Face Match, and it stays that way: NO AML screening (FitOut is not
#   an AMLA covered person and not BSP-supervised, and AML introduces In Review states no operator
#   path covers) and NO KYC expiration policy (it would silently un-approve live hosts and flip the
#   sell-gate on a live catalogue with no operator signal).
DIDIT_WORKFLOW_ID=
# DIDIT_WEBHOOK_SECRET: Didit Console -> Webhooks -> signing secret. Verifies the signature over the
#   RAW request body before the body is parsed. Didit returns this value EXACTLY ONCE, at
#   destination creation — if it is lost, registering a new destination is the only way back.
DIDIT_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# DIDIT_ENVIRONMENT: OPTIONAL, and unset is correct in dev. Which Didit application this deployment
#   is allowed to accept verdicts FROM — `live` or `sandbox`, matching the `environment` field every
#   webhook envelope carries. Defaults to `live` in production; a third value is refused, so an
#   unrecognised setting fails closed rather than defaulting to permission.
#
# ⚠ WHY IT MATTERS AND HOW IT FAILS. Outside production BOTH values are accepted, so a sandbox walk
#   against `next dev` needs nothing here. In a PRODUCTION BUILD the gate demands
#   `environment === (DIDIT_ENVIRONMENT ?? "live")` — so a sandbox webhook reaching a production
#   build with this unset is REFUSED. The refusal is authenticated-but-not-applicable, which answers
#   **200 with an audit row**, so Didit marks the delivery successful and never retries it. The host
#   then sits at `pending` forever and it looks exactly like a verdict that never arrived. The
#   reconciliation sweep is what eventually rescues that host, not a retry.
#
#   The recorded recipe for a UAT walk on a real phone prefers `next build && next start` (no HMR
#   socket, so it sidesteps the cross-origin dev guard above entirely) — and that is precisely the
#   path where this variable becomes load-bearing. Set it to `sandbox` for such a walk.
#
#   The inbound half of a two-sided guarantee: `sandbox_scenario` is rejected on live applications,
#   which stops a test hook auto-approving a real host on the way OUT. This stops a sandbox verdict
#   moving production state on the way IN.
DIDIT_ENVIRONMENT=

# --- Dev-only: reaching `next dev` from a phone (tunnel or LAN IP) ----------
# NEXT_DEV_ALLOWED_ORIGINS — OPTIONAL, and UNSET IS CORRECT for normal work. Leave it alone unless
# you are loading the DEV server from something other than localhost.
#
# WHEN YOU NEED IT: testing on a real phone. That means an ngrok/cloudflared tunnel
# (`https://<something>.ngrok-free.dev`) or your machine's LAN IP (`http://192.168.1.50:3000`).
# Both are cross-origin to `next dev`, which blocks cross-origin access to its own internals
# (/_next/*, and the /_next/webpack-hmr websocket) by default — a real guard: it stops a page in
# another tab from reading your source and bundles off a dev server bound on your machine.
# `localhost` and `*.localhost` are always allowed, which is why this has never come up before.
#
# WHAT GOES WRONG IF YOU SKIP IT — it does NOT look like a security block, so this is worth knowing:
# the HMR websocket upgrade is dropped while every HTTP asset still returns 200. The page paints,
# looks completely fine, and then IGNORES EVERY CLICK, because React never hydrates — a login submit
# falls through to a native GET (`/login?email=...&password=...`). Measured on an iPhone over a
# tunnel: 37x 200 / 87x 304 / zero failed assets, alongside 27 websocket upgrades with NO RESPONSE.
# The dev server does log `Blocked cross-origin request to Next.js dev resource /_next/webpack-hmr`
# — but on a phone you never see that log. Search terms, so the next person finds this: dev server
# loads but nothing is interactive on my phone / hydration never completes over ngrok.
#
# FORMAT: comma-separated HOSTNAMES. No scheme, NO PORT. Next matches against
# `new URL(origin).hostname`, which strips the port — so `192.168.1.50:3000` is not a stricter
# `192.168.1.50`, it is an entry that can NEVER match and will leave you debugging the same dead
# socket. Subdomain wildcards work (`*.ngrok-free.dev`); a bare `*` is rejected by Next on purpose.
# Whitespace around entries is trimmed and empty entries are dropped.
#
# DEV-ONLY, AND NEVER PRODUCTION: `allowedDevOrigins` is read only by the dev server, and
# next.config.ts additionally refuses to emit it when NODE_ENV=production — a value left set in a
# deployed environment cannot widen anything. Keep your host HERE in .env.local (gitignored), never
# in the committed next.config.ts: tunnel domains rotate and LAN IPs are personal, so hardcoding one
# widens everyone's dev server for one person's afternoon.
# NEXT_DEV_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=<your-tunnel-subdomain>.ngrok-free.dev,<your-lan-ip>
